# udev rule for the Dareu TK51G/EK75 keyboard family (VID 260D PID 0101) and
# its 2.4G dongle (VID 260D PID 0042) — open-ek75. Sold as Husky HTG200/
# HTG500/HTG800 V2 and under other brands sharing the same Dareu/TgDevice
# reference design.
#
# Install to /etc/udev/rules.d/60-ek75.rules, then:
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
# (packaging/install.sh does both for you)
#
# Both devices are configured through HID Feature Reports on their own
# /dev/hidraw* node, root-only by default. This rule hands access to the
# logged-in user for each.
#
# WHY uaccess AND NOT MODE="0666"
#
#   MODE="0666" makes the raw HID node world-readable AND world-writable for
#   every account on the machine, permanently. A hidraw node for a keyboard is
#   about as sensitive as a device gets: reading it is a full keystroke
#   logger, and writing to a *different* interface of the same device could
#   reach the boot keyboard/mouse interfaces this rule does not intend to
#   touch. On a multi-user box, or with any sandboxed/daemon account, 0666
#   is a real privilege leak — see open-m711pro's rule file for the same
#   reasoning against a mouse.
#
#   TAG+="uaccess" is the modern systemd/logind mechanism: logind attaches an
#   ACL granting access to the user of the *currently active local session*,
#   and revokes it when that session ends or is switched away. Same
#   convenience for a normal desktop, far smaller blast radius.
#
#   Requirement: systemd-logind (any mainstream desktop distro). If you are on
#   a system without logind, comment the rule out and uncomment the GROUP
#   fallback below — it is still better than 0666 because access is limited
#   to members of one group.
#
# WHY THE FILENAME STARTS WITH 60 AND NOT 99
#
#   This matters and is easy to get wrong: TAG+="uaccess" does not itself
#   grant anything. systemd applies the ACL from
#   /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/73-seat-late.rules:
#
#     TAG=="uaccess", ENV{MAJOR}!="", RUN{builtin}+="uaccess"
#
#   udev evaluates rule files in filename order, so a rule that sets the tag
#   at 99 sets it *after* 73 already tested for it. The tag lands in the udev
#   database — `udevadm info` will show CURRENT_TAGS=:uaccess: and everything
#   looks correct — but the builtin was never scheduled and the device node
#   keeps its root-only 0600 permissions. Replugging does not help, because
#   the ordering is the same on every event.
#
#   This file was originally numbered 99 and had exactly that bug. Any number
#   below 73 works; 60 matches what other hardware-access projects use
#   (60-openrgb.rules, 60-ddcutil-i2c.rules).

SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="260d", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0101", TAG+="uaccess"

# The 2.4G dongle (VID 260D PID 0042) — a second, separate physical device,
# not another interface of the keyboard above. See PROTOCOL.md's
# CLASS_DEVICE section and docs/investigations/wireless-dongle.md:
# DEV_CMD_WIRELESS_CONNECT_STATUS is built and byte-match tested, but was
# never sent until this rule existed to grant access to it. Same reasoning
# as the keyboard's own rule above applies unchanged — uaccess over MODE
# because a hidraw node is a full keystroke logger if left world-writable,
# even for a device that itself carries no keys.
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="260d", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0042", TAG+="uaccess"

# --- Fallback for systems without systemd-logind -----------------------------
# Comment out the two rules above and uncomment these, then add yourself to
# the group:  sudo usermod -aG plugdev $USER   (log out and back in)
#
#SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="260d", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0101", GROUP="plugdev", MODE="0660"
#SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", ATTRS{idVendor}=="260d", ATTRS{idProduct}=="0042", GROUP="plugdev", MODE="0660"
